The notation for `▸` is one of the few built-in notations for Lean (along with things like dot notation `foo.bar`). Therefore it is not defined as usual with the `notation` command. Nonetheless, Lean is written in Lean and you can find the Lean code implementing it. What you found so far was the code for parsing the `▸` notation, but what you wanted to find was the code for elaborating the `▸` notation.

I'm not aware of any other place where this level of detail is documented, so you will probably have to read the code. But in short it elaborates `▸` into other code, namely combinations of `Eq.rec` and `Eq.symm`. To see what `▸` is elaborated into, you can use `set_option pp.notation false` as follows:
    
    `def foo0 (α : Type) (x y z : α) (hxy : x = y) (hyz : y = z) : x = z := hyz ▸ hxy
    def foo1 (α : Type) (x y z : α) (hxy : x = y) (hzy : z = y) : x = z := hzy ▸ hxy
